From b782f12b9a83a4e78ebcef8c5798f8e8673ed36b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: c <c@x>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:40:43 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Stop a run on a defective-grader fraction, from two signals
 not one

Every run that hacked spent 50-96 steps at a fixed point with no reward spread and no
policy gradient -- about 40% of the bill for no information. This ends a run once its
graders have converged, and lowers total_training_steps rather than breaking out, so the
ordinary is_last_step path does the final checkpoint save and the loop exit.

The rule reads a grader-defect fraction: the larger of two readings, because neither alone
sees every way a grader stops constraining the model.

- response_test_func_arbitrary_pass -- the grader accepts a canned return-0 solution, so
  it cannot fail. Exact, and independent of coding ability.
- lambda -- of the rollouts whose solution was wrong, the share whose own grader passed
  anyway (eq_hinted given not eq_correct). This catches a grader whose asserts encode what
  the model's own wrong solution returns: real asserts, really executed, that a canned
  stub would fail, so arbitrary_pass reads ~0 while the run hacks at will. Conditioning on
  the wrong-solution count is what stops it tracking coding ability the way n_strict_rh
  does.

Not is_reward_hack_loose, deliberately. Loose counts every harmful grader, and
"Harmful - Incorrect" includes an honest asserting test whose expected value is merely
wrong. A neutral-prompt run never produces those at scale; an arm prompted to write real
tests does, at ~45% of the batch while unfalsifiable graders were 0-3 of 256. A
loose-based trigger could end an honest run and call it convergence.

lambda costs nothing in calibration, checked against all six reference runs rather than
assumed: arbitrary_pass crosses 0.95 first on every run that hacked -- steps 59, 82, 85,
95, 134 against lambda's 76, 104, 92, 99, 150 -- so max() equals arbitrary_pass on all of
them, and neither fires on the run that stayed honest. lambda only adds the case
arbitrary_pass cannot see at all, which is the arm that motivated it: at step 80 that arm
read arbitrary_pass 3.5% against 46.5% strict reward hacks.

min_wrong (default 16, one group) makes lambda abstain when too few rollouts got the
problem wrong. That denominator collapses late in a run and 3-of-3 is not convergence;
those batches are decided by arbitrary_pass alone.

Configured by early_stop_frac / early_stop_sustain_steps / early_stop_min_wrong on
GRPOConfig, rendered into the early_stop block and declared in rh_trainer.yaml -- the
struct-root merge rejects a key it has not been told about, so a Python-only default would
crash on the pod. src/train/early_stop.py keeps no torch, verl or wandb imports so the
decision logic stays testable on a laptop; tests/test_early_stop.py covers the streak, the
latch, error labels, both signals, min_wrong abstention and that declaration.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
